{{nihongo|'''Mārā Gyūbara Nabe'''|麻辣牛バラ鍋}} is a traditional [[Okaiken|Sanese]] meat stew, widely considered the pinnacle of Sanese comfort food. It is recognized across the archipelago as the ultimate companion to unseasoned Sanese short-grain rice. The dish consists of large, fatty cuts of beef brisket and beef tendon that are slow-braised for several hours in a heavy iron pot until meltingly tender. The resulting dark, rich broth is saturated with continental numbing peppercorns, aromatic spices imported from the Fosian continent, and indigenous dried chilies. Diners traditionally spoon the fiercely spicy, deeply savory gravy generously over their rice.

==History and Influences==
The dish is a culinary reflection of Okaiken's history as a maritime crossroads, representing a seamless synthesis of Zong, Fosia, and indigenous Sanese food cultures. 

During the late 19th and early 20th centuries, migrant laborers from  [[Zong]]  introduced the technique of red-braising tough, inexpensive cuts of meat in dark soy sauce, heavily relying on their native numbing peppercorns to mask the gamey flavor of the beef. As these laborers settled in the bustling port cities of Okaiken, they interacted with merchants from the Fosian continent. These merchants traded in warm, aromatic spices such as cumin, coriander, cloves, and black cardamom. 

The local Sanese population adapted this hybrid broth by incorporating indigenous Sanese dried chilies, transforming the flavor profile from a mild, numbing warmth into a fiercely aggressive, localized heat. Furthermore, the Sanese working class intentionally reduced the broth into a thick, concentrated gravy. This modification was made specifically to flavor large bowls of sticky, unseasoned rice, providing maximum caloric density for grueling physical labor.

==Spices and Preparation==
The preparation of Mara Gyubara Nabe is defined by its complex, multi-layered spice matrix and its prolonged cooking method. 

The flavor foundation is built by blooming Zong numbing peppercorns in hot beef tallow, which provides a tingling sensation that temporarily numbs the palate. This is immediately followed by the addition of the Fosian aromatics. A toasted, hand-ground blend of star anise, cinnamon bark, black cardamom, fennel seeds, and cloves is stirred into the fat, lending the broth its signature dark, earthy, and highly aromatic depth. The peak heat is provided by the Sanese Red Dragon chili, a native pepper dried in the harsh winter winds of the northern provinces, known for its sharp, lingering burn.

The beef brisket and tendon are first seared at a high temperature to render the fat, then submerged in the spiced soy-based liquid. The mixture is transferred to a heavy cast-iron nabe (pot) and simmered on low heat for a minimum of four hours. This slow extraction process breaks down the dense collagen within the beef tendon, which melts into the liquid and naturally thickens the gravy without the need for added starches.

==Popularization by Kurogane-ya==
[[File:Kuroganeya.png|thumb|right|250px|The original Kurogane-ya at Okami's Tatara ward.]]
While regional, home-cooked variations of the stew existed for decades, the dish was thrust into the national culinary spotlight by a single restaurant: Kurogane-ya. 

Established in 1952 in the industrial [[Tatara Ward]] of [[Okami]], Kurogane-ya originally operated as a modest, open-air eatery catering exclusively to shipyard laborers and ironworkers. These workers required cheap, high-calorie, and physically warming meals to recover from long shifts in the shipyards. The founder of Kurogane-ya standardized the spice ratio, maximizing the heat and savory umami to encourage workers to eat more rice. He also pioneered the presentation of serving the stew in individual, scorching-hot iron skillets that kept the gravy bubbling at the table.

Kurogane-ya's uncompromising spice levels and their legendary all-you-can-eat rice policy transformed the establishment into a cultural icon. Today, the original Tatara location remains a pilgrimage site for culinary enthusiasts, and the Kurogane-ya brand operates hundreds of franchised locations across Okaiken, cementing Mara Gyubara Nabe as a staple of the national diet.

==Recipe==
This is a standard household recipe for Mara Gyubara Nabe, scaled down from restaurant proportions. 

===Ingredients===
* 1 kilogram beef brisket, cut into large 5-centimeter cubes
* 500 grams beef tendon, rinsed and chopped
* 3 tablespoons beef tallow or neutral cooking oil
* 2 tablespoons Zong numbing peppercorns
* 10 whole indigenous Sanese dried chilies (substitute with Thai bird's eye chilies if unavailable)
* 5 slices fresh ginger
* 4 cloves garlic, smashed
* 1 large onion, roughly chopped
* Fosian Spice Blend: 2 star anise, 1 cinnamon stick, 3 whole cloves, 1 teaspoon fennel seeds, 1 black cardamom pod
* 100 milliliters dark soy sauce
* 50 milliliters light soy sauce
* 50 milliliters cooking wine
* 2 tablespoons fermented broad bean paste
* 1 piece rock sugar (about 30 grams)
* Water or unsalted beef stock to cover

===Instructions===
1. Place the beef brisket and tendon in a large pot of cold water. Bring to a boil and simmer for 10 minutes to remove impurities. Drain, rinse the meat, and set aside.<br>
2. Heat the beef tallow in a heavy cast-iron nabe over medium heat. Add the numbing peppercorns and dried chilies, frying gently until fragrant.<br>
3. Stir in the ginger, garlic, and fermented broad bean paste. Fry until the oil turns red and the aromatics are deeply caramelized.<br>
4. Add the blanched beef brisket and tendon to the pot, searing the meat in the spiced oil for 5 minutes.<br>
5. Pour in the dark soy sauce, light soy sauce, and cooking wine, stirring to coat the meat evenly.<br>
6. Toss in the chopped onion, rock sugar, and the complete Fosian Spice Blend. <br>
7. Pour in enough water or beef stock to completely submerge the meat. Bring the liquid to a rolling boil.<br>
8. Reduce the heat to the lowest setting, cover the nabe with a heavy lid, and braise for 4 to 5 hours. Check occasionally and add small amounts of water if the liquid reduces too quickly.<br>
9. The stew is ready when a chopstick can easily pierce the tendon and the liquid has reduced into a thick, glossy, collagen-rich gravy.<br>
10. Serve boiling hot in the iron pot, accompanied by unlimited bowls of unseasoned short-grain white rice.<br>
